1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,002
The X (Twitter) module connects your site to X in two ways.

2
00:00:05,002 --> 00:00:09,435
Visitors can sign in with their X account, and a workflow can post

3
00:00:09,435 --> 00:00:12,329
to X when something happens on your site.

4
00:00:12,329 --> 00:00:14,730
In this video, we'll set up both.

5
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:21,549
The module has two features.

6
00:00:21,549 --> 00:00:26,769
X (Twitter) Integration stores the keys of your X app, and, when Workflows

7
00:00:26,769 --> 00:00:30,359
is enabled, adds a workflow task that posts to X.

8
00:00:30,359 --> 00:00:34,470
Sign in with X (Twitter) adds a button to the login page.

9
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:39,530
Both rely on an app that you create on the X developer platform.

10
00:00:39,530 --> 00:00:44,147
Its Keys and tokens tab provides the four values the module asks for:

11
00:00:44,147 --> 00:00:49,380
the API key, the API secret key, the Access Token, and the Access Token Secret.

12
00:00:53,602 --> 00:00:57,315
Let's open Tools, Features, and search for Twitter.

13
00:00:57,315 --> 00:01:02,072
The module has two features, listed under the X (Twitter) category.

14
00:01:02,530 --> 00:01:10,019
Sign in with X (Twitter) lists X (Twitter) Integration and External Authentication as its dependencies.

15
00:01:10,019 --> 00:01:11,530
Let's enable it.

16
00:01:11,986 --> 00:01:18,134
Enabling a feature also enables its dependencies, so both X (Twitter) features show Enabled.

17
00:01:18,134 --> 00:01:21,396
Their settings pages are in the Settings menu.

18
00:01:25,594 --> 00:01:28,526
Open Settings, Integrations, X (Twitter).

19
00:01:28,526 --> 00:01:32,955
As the warning at the top of the page says, saving these settings

20
00:01:32,955 --> 00:01:34,764
might restart the site.

21
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:39,265
Enter the API key and the Access Token from the Keys and tokens

22
00:01:39,265 --> 00:01:40,549
tab of your app.

23
00:01:40,549 --> 00:01:42,940
Both fields are marked as required.

24
00:01:43,404 --> 00:01:48,414
The API secret key and the Access Token Secret are password fields, so

25
00:01:48,414 --> 00:01:50,524
their values show as dots.

26
00:01:50,524 --> 00:01:51,644
Enter both:

27
00:01:51,644 --> 00:01:56,654
with any of the four values missing, the login page shows no X button.

28
00:01:57,108 --> 00:01:58,052
Select Save.

29
00:01:58,052 --> 00:02:02,142
The page confirms that the site settings are updated, and the two secret

30
00:02:02,142 --> 00:02:05,078
fields tell you that the value is securely stored.

31
00:02:05,529 --> 00:02:09,855
If the Secrets feature is enabled, each secret field can instead use a

32
00:02:09,855 --> 00:02:12,132
secret kept in the Secrets module.

33
00:02:12,132 --> 00:02:14,579
A separate video covers that feature.

34
00:02:18,775 --> 00:02:23,351
Open Settings, Security, Authentication, Sign In with X (Twitter).

35
00:02:23,351 --> 00:02:28,755
The keys come from the integration settings, so this page has only two options.

36
00:02:29,217 --> 00:02:34,531
The CallbackPath is where X returns visitors to your site after they sign in.

37
00:02:34,531 --> 00:02:38,948
Enter /signin-x, and set the callback URL of your X app to your

38
00:02:38,948 --> 00:02:41,957
site's address followed by the same path.

39
00:02:42,416 --> 00:02:48,279
Store tokens saves the external authentication tokens for reuse, and we leave it cleared.

40
00:02:48,279 --> 00:02:52,476
Select Save, and the site might restart to apply the settings.

41
00:02:56,680 --> 00:02:58,500
Let's open the login page.

42
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:02,653
Next to the usual Log in form, a section titled Use another service

43
00:03:02,653 --> 00:03:05,270
to log in offers the X (Twitter) button.

44
00:03:05,729 --> 00:03:09,456
Selecting the button sends the visitor to X to sign in.

45
00:03:09,456 --> 00:03:13,549
X then returns the visitor to the callback path of your site.

46
00:03:17,749 --> 00:03:21,826
When X returns a visitor, the site looks for an account already linked

47
00:03:21,826 --> 00:03:23,113
to that X account.

48
00:03:23,113 --> 00:03:26,600
If it finds one and that account is allowed to sign in, the

49
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:28,049
visitor is signed in.

50
00:03:28,799 --> 00:03:33,245
Otherwise, if X provides an email that matches a local account, the page

51
00:03:33,245 --> 00:03:36,493
Link your account asks for that account's password.

52
00:03:36,493 --> 00:03:41,224
If not, the page Create a new account opens, unless the external registration

53
00:03:41,224 --> 00:03:45,499
settings disable new registrations or create accounts without asking.

54
00:03:46,249 --> 00:03:50,604
A signed-in user can also add X to an account later, from External

55
00:03:50,604 --> 00:03:52,419
Logins in the user menu.

56
00:03:56,614 --> 00:04:00,956
The module adds a task to Workflows, which is enabled on this site.

57
00:04:00,956 --> 00:04:06,368
Let's open Design, Workflows, select Create Workflow, enter a name, and select Create

58
00:04:06,368 --> 00:04:08,034
and open the designer.

59
00:04:08,498 --> 00:04:11,694
An empty workflow offers the events to start with.

60
00:04:11,694 --> 00:04:16,658
We pick Content Published Event, and the designer marks it as the start activity.

61
00:04:17,113 --> 00:04:21,526
In its settings, Content Types limits the event to the types you choose.

62
00:04:21,526 --> 00:04:25,373
We pick Blog Post, so other content does not run the workflow.

63
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:31,553
Point at the Done outcome until a plus appears, select it, and search for Twitter.

64
00:04:31,553 --> 00:04:38,313
Update X (Twitter) Status Task is listed under Social, and the designer adds it already connected.

65
00:04:38,788 --> 00:04:44,243
Its settings include Template for updating X (Twitter) status, the text of the post.

66
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:48,789
The field is required, so the activity shows an issue until you enter

67
00:04:48,789 --> 00:04:50,668
text and leave the field.

68
00:04:51,119 --> 00:04:53,120
The task has two outcomes.

69
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:58,559
Done follows when X accepts the post, and Failed follows when X returns an error.

70
00:04:59,014 --> 00:05:03,610
The designer keeps your changes as a draft, and a draft does not run.

71
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:08,634
Select Publish, and from then on publishing a Blog Post sends the text to X.

72
00:05:10,634 --> 00:05:14,415
With the keys of your X app saved, visitors can sign in with

73
00:05:14,415 --> 00:05:16,535
X, and workflows can post to X.

74
00:05:16,535 --> 00:05:18,024
Thanks for watching.

